            <ArticleTitle>Genome-first approach diagnosed Cabezas syndrome via novel CUL4B mutation detection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>16045</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/hgv.2016.45</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cabezas syndrome is a syndromic form of X-linked intellectual disability primarily characterized by a short stature, hypogonadism and abnormal gait, with other variable features resulting from mutations in the CUL4B gene. Here, we report a clinically undiagnosed 5-year-old male with severe intellectual disability. A genome-first approach using targeted exome sequencing identified a novel nonsense mutation [NM_003588.3:c.2698G&gt;T, p.(Glu900*)] in the last coding exon of CUL4B, thus diagnosing this patient with Cabezas syndrome.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
